---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="How a Heatmap Can Mislead You"
  description="How a grid-based heatmap can suggest different best locations without changing the underlying demand"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        <!-- Max size, grid 72 px and 66 points, in lambda (4 points similar to a lambda constellation) -->
        Imagine this is a map of coffee demand in Manhattan. Every dot is someone
        looking for coffee. So this heatmap should at least point to where demand
        is stronger. But not so fast.
      </p>
      <p>
        Watch what happens if we move the grid... just a little bit. Now the
        best location is here instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two people can analyze the exact same data, both use perfectly valid
        methods, and still reach different conclusions. Who's wrong?
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Maybe nobody is wrong. Because the analysis didn't start when we counted
        the dots. It started earlier. When we decided what counts as an area.
      </p>
      <p>
        A small square grid. Or a larger grid. Hexagons. Postal codes... They
        look different, but they all do the same thing: they draw lines around
        the dots. And to see why that matters, let's push it to an extreme.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the same dots, one boundary can split the demand almost evenly.
        Another boundary can make the left side win. Another one can make the
        right side win. Same dots. Different grouping. Different story.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the question is not only: what does the map say? It is also: what did
        we make the map say when we chose these boundaries?
      </p>
      <p>
        And this is not just about coffee shops. We see maps like this
        everywhere: election results, house prices, Covid cases, population
        density. So should we stop using them?
        <pre>
          https://www.anychart.com/blog/2024/11/08/us-election-maps/
          https://www.google.com/search?q=house+prices+by+state+us+public+results
          https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/COVID-19_pandemic_by_country_and_territory#/media/File:COVID-19_Outbreak_World_Map_Total_Deaths_per_Capita.svg
          https://studio.foursquare.com/public/5c653399-e79c-4aee-9891-df378da2dd43
        </pre>
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        No. Maps like this can still be useful. But we need to stop asking only:
        which area wins? We also need to ask: how easily does the winner change?
      </p>
      <p>
        In this first case, small changes in the grid keep changing the winner.
        One grid says here. Another grid says there. And after a few versions,
        the map is full of different answers. That is a fragile result.
      </p>
      <p>
        But now look at this second case. The exact winning cell may move a
        little, but the same general area keeps coming back. Again. And again.
        That is a much stronger signal. That is the idea of stability. A
        conclusion that survives more than one map.
      </p>
      <p>
        But even then, there is another problem: what if we cannot move the
        boundaries at all?
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Instead of drawing our own grid, we can aggregate the same points by
        postal code, by district, or by state. Those boundaries are fixed. We
        cannot just move them around.
      </p>
      <p>
        But sometimes, you can still change the scale. Look at the same question
        from far away. Then zoom in. A country becomes states. A state becomes
        cities. A city becomes neighborhoods.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the story changes completely when you zoom in, be careful. But if the
        same general pattern survives at different scales, the conclusion
        becomes easier to trust.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        But even changing scale does not solve everything. Because boundaries
        have another problem: they are sharp, but reality is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        A customer just across the line is still nearby. On the map, the
        boundary looks sharp. But on the street, there is no wall there. No real
        break in the city. The line separates areas on the map, not reality on
        the ground.
      </p>
      <p>
        So a hotspot split by that line is still one hotspot. So instead of
        treating every cell as completely isolated, we can smooth the map. Each
        area looks not only at itself, but also at its neighbors.
      </p>
      <p>
        But then you might ask: why use cells at all? Why not remove the grid
        completely, and use a smooth heatmap? That can help. But it is not
        magic.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now you have to choose how far each point spreads. Too little smoothing,
        and the map still looks noisy. Too much smoothing, and different
        hotspots can melt into one.
      </p>
      <p>
        So again, the goal is not to remove every choice. The goal is to check
        whether the conclusion survives reasonable choices.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        So whether we move the grid, change the scale, smooth with neighbors, or
        use a continuous heatmap, we are not removing choices. We are testing
        whether the conclusion survives them.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        And this is where it stops being just about maps. Because the real issue
        is not the map. It is the grouping. Every time we group data, we create
        boundaries.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sometimes those boundaries are drawn on a map. Sometimes they are
        categories. Sometimes they are dates. They may not be physical walls,
        but they still decide what gets grouped together.
      </p>
      <p>
        In a stock chart, for example, the boundary can be a line in time. For
        example, take the same monthly stock movements. Measure the year from
        January to December, and you get one story. Move the year boundary from
        December to November, and the story can change. Same data. Different
        cut. Different conclusion.
        <!-- 2018! Mover a la izquierda. -->
      </p>
      <p>
        There are many versions of this warning. Sometimes it is a map that
        changes its answer. Sometimes it is a chart that changes its story.
        Sometimes it is a statistic that looks obvious, until you ask how the
        groups were made.
      </p>
      <p>
        Different examples. Same warning: aggregation can hide as much as it
        reveals.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        But most of the time, we are not the ones doing the analysis. We just
        see the final map, the final chart, or the headline. And that does not
        mean everything is fake. But it does mean something important: the
        analysis is making choices for us.
      </p>
      <p>
        It chooses the boundaries. It chooses the scale. It chooses what gets
        averaged together. And each choice can change what feels obvious. A
        state can look like one single political opinion, even if most of the
        result comes from one city.
      </p>
      <p>
        A postal code can look expensive, even if only one town inside it is
        driving the average. A chart can make a year look terrible, just because
        of where we decided the year begins and ends.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the point is not to stop trusting aggregations. It is to remember
        that they are not just summaries of the data. They are summaries of the
        data, after someone decided how to group it.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now try it yourself. Move the grid. Change the scale. Smooth the map.
        Watch how the answer changes. Same data. Different boundaries. Different
        story.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
